{:edb/db-before-t 564 :edb/db-after-t 565 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533877 50 #inst "2026-10-05T06:46:23.082Z" 13194139533877 true] [17592186083147 1020 #edb/ref 17592186262917 13194139533877 true] [17592186083147 1020 #edb/ref 17592186262924 13194139533877 true] [17592186083217 1020 #edb/ref 17592186262910 13194139533877 true] [17592186083221 1020 #edb/ref 17592186262927 13194139533877 true] [17592186083448 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533877 true] [17592186083448 1145 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-8,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 4,$ and $\\pm 8.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $10,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 5,$ and $\\pm 10.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{10}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{10}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{4}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{10}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{8}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{8}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{8}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{8}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{10}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{2}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}, \\\\ \\pm 4, & \\pm 2, & \\pm \\frac{4}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{5}, \\\\ \\pm 8, & \\pm 4, & \\pm \\frac{8}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that several potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{10},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{5},\\,\\pm \\frac{2}{5},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{4}{5},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm \\frac{8}{5},\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 8.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $20$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 10=20.$)" 13194139533877 true] [17592186262910 1017 #edb/ref 17592186262911 13194139533877 true] [17592186262910 1089 "q-112877" 13194139533877 true] [17592186262910 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533877 true] [17592186262910 1100 "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=-6{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-9x+9?$" 13194139533877 true] [17592186262910 1113 #uuid "0ced084c-e5cc-510a-ae57-0a31b5977172" 13194139533877 true] [17592186262910 1145 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $9$ and the leading coefficient is $-6.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }9}{\\text{factor of }-6}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-2$ does not take the above form. The value $-2$ can be interpreted as a fraction $\\frac{2}{-1},$ and the numerator $(2)$ is not a factor of $9.$" 13194139533877 true] [17592186262911 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262912 13194139533877 true] [17592186262911 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262913 13194139533877 true] [17592186262911 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262914 13194139533877 true] [17592186262911 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262915 13194139533877 true] [17592186262911 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262916 13194139533877 true] [17592186262911 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533877 true] [17592186262911 1041 "selection" 13194139533877 true] [17592186262911 1104 #edb/ref 17592186262916 13194139533877 true] [17592186262911 1110 #uuid "0036bd73-610c-537a-a14