st out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{4}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{8}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{8}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{8}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{16}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{16}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{16}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\pm 4, & \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, \\\\ \\pm 8, & \\pm 4, & \\pm 2, \\\\ \\pm 16, & \\pm 8, & \\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 8,\\,\\pm 16.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $14$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 7=14.$)" 13194139533877 true] [17592186262918 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262919 13194139533877 true] [17592186262918 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262920 13194139533877 true] [17592186262918 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262921 13194139533877 true] [17592186262918 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262922 13194139533877 true] [17592186262918 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262923 13194139533877 true] [17592186262918 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533877 true] [17592186262918 1041 "selection" 13194139533877 true] [17592186262918 1104 #edb/ref 17592186262922 13194139533877 true] [17592186262918 1110 #uuid "65bcd3d2-be94-577a-b059-0f400fb19888" 13194139533877 true] [17592186262919 1034 "28" 13194139533877 true] [17592186262919 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533877 true] [17592186262919 1111 #uuid "adda1d6a-7132-596e-975a-6b35bfde7502" 13194139533877 true] [17592186262920 1034 "15" 13194139533877 true] [17592186262920 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533877 true] [17592186262920 1111 #uuid "685a3ca8-3740-5aeb-a97a-e7d1b6fa760d" 13194139533877 true] [17592186262921 1034 "7" 13194139533877 true] [17592186262921 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533877 true] [17592186262921 1111 #uuid "cc17ab0e-4d7f-5fb4-b245-11b963aa0357" 13194139533877 true] [17592186262922 1034 "14" 13194139533877 true] [17592186262922 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533877 true] [17592186262922 1111 #uuid "873eaede-5120-5202-8ab0-6ff827aae300" 13194139533877 true] [17592186262923 1034 "5" 13194139533877 true] [17592186262923 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533877 true] [17592186262923 1111 #uuid "88949f27-0690-5d39-bd5b-4325a8444108" 13194139533877 true] [17592186262924 1017 #edb/ref 17592186262925 13194139533877 true] [17592186262924 1089 "q-270438" 13194139533877 true] [17592186262924 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533877 true] [17592186262924 1100 "According to the rational root theorem, the polynomial $f(x)=26{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}+4x+4$ has{{field-1}} *potential* rational roots." 13194139533877 true] [17592186262924 1113 #uuid "eef2063a-ea31-5e8a-8994-03247a623cca" 13194139533877 true] [17592186262924 1145 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $4,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$,  $\\pm 2,$ and $\\pm 4.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $26,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$, $\\pm 2$, $\\pm 13,$ and $\\pm 26.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{13}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{26}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{13}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{26}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{4}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{13}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{26}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to their lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{13}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{26}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{2}{13}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{13}, \\\\ \\pm 4, & \\pm 2, & \\pm \\frac{4}{13}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{26},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{13},\\,\\pm \\frac{2}{13},\\,\\pm \\frac{4}{13},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 4.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $16$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 8=16.$)" 13194139533877 true] [17592186262925 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262926 13194139533877 true] [17592186262925 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533877 true] [17592186262925 1041 "field-1" 13194139533877 true] [17592186262925 1104 #edb/ref 17592186262926 13194139533877 true] [17592186262925 1110 #uuid "e4b64a15-abf7-58be-9490-19f21b3eaf93" 13194139533877 true] [17592186262926 1034 "16" 13194139533877 true] [17592186262926 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533877 true] [17592186262926 1111 #uuid "148c24da-df45-50c7-b327-bf3cb25c2afd" 13194139533877 true] [17592186262927 1017 #edb/ref 17592186262928 13194139533877 true] [17592186262927 1089 "q-83654" 13194139533877 true] [17592186262927 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533877 true] [17592186262927 1100 "Given that the polynomial $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+5x-3$ has a root in the interval $(0,\\frac{3}{2}),$ find the sum of the **distinct** real roots of $f(x).$" 13194139533877 true] [17592186262927 1113 #uuid "02c855c8-9507-553d-b37d-de70c503e21a" 13194139533877 true] [17592186262927 1145 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-3$ and the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-3}{\\text{factor of }2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\pm \\frac{3}{2},\\pm 1,\\pm 3.\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(0,\\frac{3}{2}),$ are $\\frac{1}{2}$ and $1.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x-\\frac{1}{2})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ | $1$ | $5$ | $-3$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $1$ | $1$ | $3$ |\n|  | $2$ | $2$ | $6$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x-\\frac{1}{2})$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-\\frac{1}{2})(2{x}^{2}+2x+6).\n$$\n\nNow we must see if the polynomial $g(x)=2{x}^{2}+2x+6$ has real roots. For this, we must find the value of its discriminant $D.$ Note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(2)^{2}-4(2)(6) \\\\  & =4-48 \\\\  & =-44.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D<0,$ the polynomial $g(x)$ doesn't have real roots. \n\nTherefore, the only root of $f(x)$ is $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139533877 true] [17592186262928 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262929 13194139533877 true] [17592186262928 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262930 13194139533877 true] [17592186262928 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262931 13194139533877 true] [17592186262928 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262932 13194139533877 true] [17592186262928 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262933 13194139533877 true] [17592186262928 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533877 true] [17592186262928 1041 "selection" 13194139533877 true] [17592186262928 1104 #edb/ref 17592186262931 13194139533877 true] [17592186262928 1110 #uuid "9bba5c28-2c25-5006-b